{"builder":"Goal: make the jsonl-lint CLI (packages/jsonl-lint) accept several files and stdin, as GitHub issue https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104 asks. Make the whole change in one pull request.\n\nCurrent state: packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js reads only process.argv[2]. It prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` (newlines in the error become spaces) for each bad row. It exits 0 when the file is clean, 1 when a row is bad, and 2 when no argument is given or the file can't be read (message on stderr). The checking itself is `lint(text)` in packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js. The tests are packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js and packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js, and they use spawnSync on the bin. The docs are packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. CI is .github/workflows/test.yml, which runs `npm ci` and then `npm test` on Node 22.\n\nSteps:\n1. Rewrite packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js to take `<file> [<file> ...]`:\n   - No file arguments: print a usage line (e.g. `usage: jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`) to stderr and exit 2.\n   - More than one `-` argument: this is a usage error. Print a message to stderr and exit 2. Do this check before reading anything.\n   - Otherwise go through the arguments in the order given. An argument of `-` means read all of stdin (e.g. `readFileSync(0, 'utf8')`) and report it under the name `-`. Any other argument is read as a file path. For each bad row, print `<name>:<line>: <error>` to stdout in exactly the current format, using the argument exactly as given.\n   - If a file can't be read, print `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` to stderr, remember the failure, and keep going with the remaining files. Their bad rows must still be printed.\n   - Exit code: 2 if any file couldn't be read. Otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad. Otherwise 0. A read failure wins over bad rows.\n   - Output for a single file must stay byte-for-byte the same as before.\n2. Do not touch `lint(text)` in packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js. Leave that file as it is.\n3. Add node:test tests to packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js. Keep the existing tests passing. Each case below needs at least one test:\n   - two clean files → exit 0, empty stdout\n   - one bad file among several clean files → exit 1, and only the bad file's `<file>:<line>:` lines are printed\n   - stdin via `-`, clean → exit 0. Use spawnSync's `input` option.\n   - stdin via `-`, bad → exit 1, and the lines start with `-:<line>: `\n   - a missing file among good ones (put one bad file among the good ones too) → exit 2, stderr names the missing file, and the bad rows of the other files are still on stdout\n   - two `-` arguments → exit 2, message on stderr\n   - no arguments → exit 2, usage on stderr\n4. Update packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. Document the usage `node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...]`. Say that files are checked in order, that `-` reads stdin and is reported as `-`, and that only one `-` is allowed. Update the exit codes section to say: 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, and 2 if no file is named, `-` is given twice, or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported).\n5. Run `npm test` at the repo root and make sure it passes. Also run `npm ci && npm test` to mirror CI.\n6. Open one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. Its body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Wait for the test workflow on the PR head to finish. If it fails, fix the cause and push again until it is green.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run every check below from the repository root. Let BIN=packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js. Create fixtures in a temp dir T:\n- T/a.jsonl = `{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n`\n- T/b.jsonl = `{\"c\":3}\\n`\n- T/bad.jsonl = `{\"a\":1}\\n{bad}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n{oops}\\n`\n\nFor each command, record the exit status, stdout and stderr.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\"\nRun: `node $BIN T/bad.jsonl` (compare with `node -e` calling lint from packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js on that file's text). Then run `cp T/bad.jsonl T/bad2.jsonl; node $BIN T/a.jsonl T/bad.jsonl T/b.jsonl T/bad2.jsonl`.\nPASS if both of these hold:\n- The single-file run prints exactly `T/bad.jsonl:2: <msg>` and `T/bad.jsonl:4: <msg>`, where each <msg> is that row's JSON.parse message with newlines turned into spaces.\n- The multi-file run prints exactly those two lines for T/bad.jsonl, followed by the same two lines for T/bad2.jsonl, in that order, and nothing for the clean files. Each file name appears exactly as typed.\nFAIL if any of these happen:\n- a file is skipped\n- the order is wrong\n- the format differs from `<file>:<line>: <error>`\n- clean files produce output\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\"\nRun:\n- `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n{bad}\\n' | node $BIN T/a.jsonl -`\n- `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n' | node $BIN -`\n- `printf '{\"a\":1}\\n' | node $BIN - T/a.jsonl -`\nPASS if all of these hold:\n- The first run prints exactly one stdout line, starting `-:2: `, and exits 1.\n- The second run exits 0 with empty stdout.\n- The third run exits 2 with a message on stderr.\nFAIL otherwise.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\"\nRun:\n- `node $BIN T/a.jsonl T/b.jsonl`\n- `node $BIN T/a.jsonl T/bad.jsonl T/b.jsonl`\n- `node $BIN`\n- `node $BIN T/a.jsonl T/missing.jsonl T/bad.jsonl`\nPASS if all of these hold:\n- The first run exits 0.\n- The second run exits 1.\n- The third run exits 2 with non-empty stderr.\n- The fourth run exits 2, its stderr mentions T/missing.jsonl, and its stdout still contains the `T/bad.jsonl:2:` and `T/bad.jsonl:4:` lines.\nFAIL otherwise.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\"\nRun `git diff --exit-code <merge-base with main>..HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js` and `npm test --workspace packages/jsonl-lint`.\nPASS if the diff is empty, or at least the lint function's body and signature are identical, and the existing lint tests pass.\nFAIL if lint(text) was changed.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\"\nRun `npm test --workspace packages/jsonl-lint`, and read the test files under packages/jsonl-lint/test/ and packages/jsonl-lint/README.md.\nPASS if all of these hold:\n- The tests pass.\n- They use node:test.\n- There is at least one test for each of these 7 cases:\n  - two clean files\n  - one bad file among several\n  - stdin via `-` clean\n  - stdin via `-` bad\n  - a missing file among good ones\n  - two `-` arguments\n  - no arguments\n- The README shows the `<file> [<file> ...]` usage and describes `-` as stdin.\nFAIL if any case is missing, the tests fail, or the README lacks the multi-file/stdin usage.\n\nCheck 6. Expect line: \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\"\nRun:\n- `gh pr list --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test --search 104 --state all`\n- `gh pr view <n> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test --json body,headRefOid`\n- `npm ci && npm test` at the head\n- `gh pr checks <n> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test`\nPASS if all of these hold:\n- Exactly one PR carries the change.\n- Its body contains `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`.\n- `npm test` exits 0 locally.\n- The test workflow run for the head SHA concluded success.\nFAIL otherwise.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Check that the single-file output is byte-identical to the base commit's output by running the base bin on the same fixtures.\n- Check that a read failure combined with bad rows gives 2, not 1.\n- Look for a test that asserts stdout order across files."}